 "drivingQuestion": "Why does the mishna need to state this?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "the clause teaches that the fifth does not hold up redemption",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shabbat.127b.20",
   "daf": "127b",
   "sentenceId": 19,
   "he": "וּמַעֲשֵׂר שֵׁנִי וְכוּ׳. פְּשִׁיטָא! לָא צְרִיכָא שֶׁנָּתַן אֶת הַקֶּרֶן וְלֹא נָתַן אֶת הַחוֹמֶשׁ. הָא קָא מַשְׁמַע לַן דְּאֵין חוֹמֶשׁ מְעַכֵּב.",
   "en": "We learned in the mishna: One may move second tithe that was redeemed. The Gemara asks: This is obvious. The Gemara answers: «It was necessary» to teach this halakha «only in» a case where the consecrated property was not completely redeemed, i.e., «where one gave» payment for «the principal,» the value of the tithe, «but he did not give» payment for «the fifth» that he must add when redeeming items that he consecrated. «And» the mishna «teaches us» that failure to add «the fifth does not invalidate» the redemption, and the second tithe assumes non-sacred status from the moment that one pays the principal."
  }
 ],
